1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
what occurred is there's a shift in demand and a curve.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:06,280
Okay, so usually we economists don't like to just go

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,200
and assume that preferences randomly change.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:11,840
Now if you're talking about supply there, they can go

5
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:14,180
and look at things like input prices and say, ah, well,

6
00:00:14,520 --> 00:00:16,520
we'll only like we could say the supply chains

7
00:00:16,520 --> 00:00:18,380
of input prices change if it's demand.

8
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:21,640
Then if you've got some very clear predictors of demand

9
00:00:21,640 --> 00:00:24,780
like demand for skiing in the winter,

10
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:26,500
then you might say, okay, that's fine.

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:27,740
We'll go and we'll put in shell

12
00:00:27,740 --> 00:00:30,700
like these identifiable weather shifters into the model

13
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,200
and then we can go and test it.

14
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,420
But the idea that someone might just wake up and say,

15
00:00:35,420 --> 00:00:36,860
I just don't like skiing anymore, man,

16
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:38,420
or that a whole country could wake up.

17
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:39,560
We don't like skiing anymore.

18
00:00:40,080 --> 00:00:42,620
That idea is one that economists normally resist

19
00:00:42,620 --> 00:00:45,380
and empirical work in economics usually doesn't say stuff

20
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:45,800
like that.

21
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:47,040
All right.

22
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:50,900
So once you put in stable preferences,

23
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,000
then we can definitely go and empirically test, basic theory.

24
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,320
And then another thing, another assumption,

25
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,840
so besides the assumption of stable preferences,

26
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:03,600
if we go further and assume

27
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:05,220
that people have selfish preferences,

28
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:08,980
then we can go even further towards testing basic micro.

29
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:13,240
So if we assume that people are selfish, then we can go

30
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:19,780
and test theories like what will landlords do if you go

31
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:22,060
and impose rent control, right?

32
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:24,840
Because suppose that landlords were totally unselfish

33
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:25,760
and just wanted to help people.

34
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:29,160
So all they do is they wake up and say, I want to go

35
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:31,760
and provide housing for people who need houses.

36
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:34,500
If that were the way that landlords thought and then you go

37
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,420
and impose rent control, there's no reason for them

38
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:39,000
to withdraw units from the market.

39
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:43,940
So normally economists are assuming selfishness, right?

40
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:47,800
And if you think about that Adam Smith quote

41
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:49,200
that economists love to invoke,

42
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:52,860
not from benevolence does the baker bake his bread, but from?

43
00:01:52,860 --> 00:01:56,120
Yes, his own regard to his own self-interest.

44
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:57,600
Economists love quoting this.

45
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:01,620
And yet I've seen the very same economists I think,

46
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:04,940
some when accused of assuming selfishness say, oh, no, no,

47
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:06,060
no, I don't assume selfishness.

48
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:07,260
No, economists never do that.

49
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:08,080
What are you talking about?

50
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:12,060
Then why are you always quoting Adam Smith, right?

51
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:13,940
And furthermore, if you don't assume selfishness,

52
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:15,720
then a whole bunch of the things

53
00:02:15,720 --> 00:02:18,160
that economists normally say don't even follow anymore.

54
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:20,320
You know, the idea that if you don't,

55
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:22,620
that if people don't get paid for making food,

56
00:02:22,620 --> 00:02:24,200
that they'll just give up on it and won't do it.

57
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:26,780
Does that actually make sense?

58
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,540
I mean, don't you need selfishness to make that work?

59
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:29,020
Yeah.

60
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,040
This might be a question for after class,

61
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:34,280
but I've always understood self-interest and selfish

62
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:35,420
to be separate things.

63
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,000
You're using the terms of.

64
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,160
Yeah, I mean, I would say that they're, you know,

65
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:42,980
I would say the relationship between selfish and, you know,

66
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:45,200
selfish and self-interested is like the relationship

67
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,140
between being frugal and being cheap.

68
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:50,540
One has a negative connotation,

69
00:02:50,540 --> 00:02:52,660
but they're fundamentally the same thing.

70
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:55,580
So if you were to say this person is completely

71
00:02:55,580 --> 00:02:57,560
self-interested, but he's not at all selfish,

72
00:02:58,500 --> 00:02:59,980
I don't know what that person is like.

73
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:02,160
I would just say that like, what does that mean?

74
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,620
Just like you said, the person is totally frugal,

75
00:03:04,620 --> 00:03:05,560
but in no way cheap.

76
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:08,540
So, yeah, that's my view.

77
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:11,380
Again, so I know that, you know, so like the dictionary,

78
00:03:11,560 --> 00:03:12,820
again, I think it will basically just add

79
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:13,980
on the negative connotation.

80
00:03:13,980 --> 00:03:17,500
So selfishness is a negative term that we use for someone

81
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:19,300
who is being too self-interested.

82
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:21,140
But, of course, if you as economists assume complete

83
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:23,460
that you're completely self-interested, there's no room

84
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:26,020
to be any more self-interested than completely self-interested.

85
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:30,140
So if anyone is selfish, it would have

86
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:32,020
to be a completely self-interested person, I think.

87
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:32,980
Yes?

88
00:03:32,980 --> 00:03:36,140
So I think, so by saying selfish, you have to give some kind

89
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:37,900
of content to that to make it appear.

90
00:03:37,900 --> 00:03:38,940
Yeah, yes, yes, of course.

91
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:39,420
Well, so.

92
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:40,340
You have to have some sense

93
00:03:40,340 --> 00:03:42,860
of narrow material self-interest or money.

94
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:43,140
Right, right.

95
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:45,180
Well, so like what did Adam Smith have in mind

96
00:03:45,180 --> 00:03:46,300
when he said that?

97
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:50,180
He might have had in mind some kind of material self-interest.

98
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:51,740
Yeah, seems likely.

99
00:03:51,740 --> 00:03:53,340
I mean, in fact, might.

100
00:03:53,340 --> 00:03:54,740
Like, almost certainly.

101
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:56,260
Like, what else would you mean by, well,

102
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:58,860
why is the baker getting up at 5 in the morning to bake bread?

103
00:03:59,260 --> 00:04:00,860
A, because he wants to make some money.

104
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:03,580
But the quote is saying that it's not in the interest,

105
00:04:03,820 --> 00:04:05,820
it's not the interest of the person buying the bread that's

106
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:06,300
at stake.

107
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:08,620
That person may have someone else's interests,

108
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:10,340
not his own material self-interest.

109
00:04:10,780 --> 00:04:14,460
And he has a, he's not doing it for the person buying.

110
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:15,060
Right.

111
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:17,300
But he might be doing it to help his church.

112
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:17,500
Yes.

113
00:04:17,500 --> 00:04:19,940
Or some poor person on the street.

114
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:24,060
I mean, anything's possible, but normally not.

115
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:25,620
Right, you know, if you said his child,

116
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:26,620
that would make a lot of sense.

117
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:28,820
And we're going to get into the, like,

118
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:30,580
sort of narrowing down self-interest.

119
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:32,220
But again, there's, you know, there is,

120
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:33,460
there's a normal English word,

121
00:04:33,460 --> 00:04:35,300
normal English word, self-interested.

122
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,980
And most of us have very little trouble going

123
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:40,300
and figuring out whether an action qualifies.

124
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,620
Although, of course, you can say, well,

125
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:44,900
what was Mother Teresa really trying to do?

126
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,300
All right.

127
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:48,820
Right. And, of course, you can imagine

128
00:04:48,820 --> 00:04:50,660
that she actually was stocking away tons of money

129
00:04:50,660 --> 00:04:52,020
in a secret Swiss bank account.

130
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:53,860
And was living high off the hog.

131
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:57,180
Right. And my understanding is Christopher Hitchens does have a

132
00:04:57,180 --> 00:04:59,260
book where he kind of accused her of something

133
00:04:59,260 --> 00:05:02,340
in that ballpark, not quite, but be, like,

134
00:05:02,340 --> 00:05:04,700
like actually not be, really being all that great.

135
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:05,500
All right.

136
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:07,260
But, but again, so you have an idea.

137
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:08,260
Well, here's a book that says

138
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:09,420
that Mother Teresa wasn't really,

139
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:10,700
wasn't really all that great.

140
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:12,860
You have some idea about what would be in that book, right?

141
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:16,820
Right. And, and if, if you had a friend and you're,

142
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:18,380
like you're going and shopping for a present.

143
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:20,900
And they say, well, like I'm just doing this

144
00:05:20,900 --> 00:05:21,780
because I'm selfish.

145
00:05:22,420 --> 00:05:23,860
Like, so, well, you're buying a present

146
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:25,020
for orphans because you're selfish?

147
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,820
Why? It's like, well, I'm doing it in order

148
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:28,780
to impress an employer who really likes this philanthropy.

149
00:05:28,780 --> 00:05:29,980
He's, all right, well, that's a good story.

150
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:32,660
But on the other hand, if you have some story, like,

151
00:05:32,860 --> 00:05:34,860
I'm doing it because I think I'm, I'm trying

152
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:37,460
to save the orphans because maybe one day they'll save me.

153
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:40,860
Like, yeah, that sounds like a real long shot.

154
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:43,460
I guess it's possible, but that just doesn't ring true.

155
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:43,700
All right.

156
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:46,540
But we'll consider it a bit more as we go on.

157
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:48,900
So, anyway, if we add on selfishness, then we got a lot

158
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:50,660
of other testable implications.

159
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:53,900
Now, once we move from your basic consumer theory

160
00:05:54,180 --> 00:05:57,220
into expected utility theory, which we learned two weeks ago,

161
00:05:57,820 --> 00:05:59,900
then we got a lot of other testable implications.

162
00:05:59,900 --> 00:06:02,900
Remember, we have things like no discontinuities

163
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:04,820
between a zero probability of risk

164
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:06,380
and a 1% probability of risk.

165
00:06:07,380 --> 00:06:08,500
That's an empirical claim.

166
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:13,220
That people won't pay more to move from one to zero

167
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:16,780
than they will to pay to move from two to 1% of a risk.

168
00:06:17,460 --> 00:06:20,460
Right? And also says there'll be no point in advertising.

169
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:22,100
We can eliminate this risk completely.

170
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:26,500
That won't be any more appealing than saying we can go

171
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:29,500
and move the risk down by 1 percentage point.

172
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:31,620
So, you're already, you know, so two to one versus one to zero.

173
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:31,860
All right.

174
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:34,100
And again, if you think actually that seems like it would work.

175
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:35,860
A lot of people really like that.

176
00:06:35,860 --> 00:06:38,580
You have no risk.

177
00:06:38,580 --> 00:06:42,700
So, I have no downside at all now.

178
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:43,940
None. All right.

179
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:44,620
Now I can sleep.

180
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:46,780
As long as it's 1% I couldn't sleep.

181
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:49,180
2% and 1 almost the same.

182
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:52,700
But going from one to zero, then I sleep like a baby.

183
00:06:53,220 --> 00:06:53,420
All right.

184
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:55,380
So, sometimes people feel this way.

185
00:06:55,620 --> 00:06:58,820
So, at least allegedly we might investigate that.

186
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:00,300
All right.

187
00:07:00,300 --> 00:07:00,580
So, anyway.

188
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:02,660
So, you got this rapidly expanding literature.

189
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:05,660
Actually, so now I'd say it's not expanding as rapidly anymore

190
00:07:05,660 --> 00:07:06,700
because it's gotten so much bigger.

191
00:07:07,700 --> 00:07:09,940
So, once something is really big then it's hard to expand

192
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:10,980
as rapidly as it used to.

193
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,860
But anyway, let's just call it a big literature.

194
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:14,940
Often called behavioral economics.

195
00:07:15,460 --> 00:07:17,180
Which is itself a really weird term, right?

196
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:19,540
It's like, well, isn't all economics about behavior?

197
00:07:20,420 --> 00:07:23,820
Yeah, but we work with the phrases

198
00:07:23,820 --> 00:07:25,540
that other people have already given us even

199
00:07:25,540 --> 00:07:26,860
if they aren't really very good.

200
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:31,020
I mean, I think that in an ideal world economics would be called

201
00:07:31,020 --> 00:07:32,700
sociology, the study of society.

202
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:34,220
But that ain't going to happen.

203
00:07:34,220 --> 00:07:39,100
And it would be a marketing disaster if it did, right?

204
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:40,900
Just like the marketing disaster

205
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:42,700
of declaring Pluto no longer a planet.

206
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:45,780
Why would astronomers do that?

207
00:07:46,260 --> 00:07:48,220
It's one of the worst possible things they could do

208
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:51,500
for their field to go and take away a beloved frozen ice planet

209
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:52,380
away for children.

210
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:54,700
But they did it because they're like, I don't know,

211
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:57,180
they have no sense of marketing at all I guess.

212
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:57,940
All right.

213
00:07:59,220 --> 00:08:01,740
Now, the product of behavioral economics is a long list

214
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:03,060
of what are called anomalies.

215
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:04,300
Anomalies.

216
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:05,620
All right.

217
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:09,300
Now, a quick version of anomalies is just weird stuff

218
00:08:09,300 --> 00:08:15,620
we found, but the technical idea is that this is robust evidence

219
00:08:16,100 --> 00:08:18,940
that people sometimes are violating basic axioms

220
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:19,500
of choice theory.

221
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:23,260
Robust evidence that people are sometimes violating your basic

222
00:08:23,260 --> 00:08:24,220
axioms of choice theory.

223
00:08:24,740 --> 00:08:25,620
All right.

224
00:08:26,140 --> 00:08:29,260
And any of you, I'm pretty sure the column is long gone,

225
00:08:29,260 --> 00:08:31,060
but Richard Thaler used to have a column in the Journal

226
00:08:31,060 --> 00:08:34,340
of Economic Perspectives called Anomalies,

227
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:37,460
where each article would be something that we see

228
00:08:37,460 --> 00:08:39,740
in the real world that seems to be predicted

229
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:41,420
to not exist by economic theory.

230
00:08:43,420 --> 00:08:43,700
All right.

231
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:48,260
Now, these violations of choice theory are also often called

232
00:08:48,260 --> 00:08:48,900
irrationality.

233
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:51,780
So, irrationality.

234
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:58,220
Right. And especially if you are an old economist.

235
00:08:58,220 --> 00:09:01,260
An economist, though, so old that you got your PhD before,

236
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:02,260
say, 1973.

237
00:09:02,940 --> 00:09:03,060
All right.

238
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:04,540
There's not that many left anymore,

239
00:09:04,540 --> 00:09:05,300
but they're still around.

240
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:10,220
So, if you got your PhD before then, then you are likely to go

241
00:09:10,220 --> 00:09:12,700
and just treat these things as being exactly the same.

242
00:09:13,620 --> 00:09:14,020
All right.

243
00:09:14,740 --> 00:09:17,980
So, and what's the key is if you got your PhD

244
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:19,860
before the Rational Expeditions Revolution,

245
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,300
then you just think of rationality as being things

246
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:23,580
like having a transitive behavior.

247
00:09:23,580 --> 00:09:27,460
Or it could also be things like counting sunk costs

248
00:09:27,460 --> 00:09:28,420
when you make your decisions.

249
00:09:29,100 --> 00:09:30,820
That would be another classic example.

250
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:31,700
All right.

251
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:33,740
So, how many people here have reached the point

252
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:35,420
where you walk out of movies appropriately?

253
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:38,900
Yes. How many are not quite there yet?

254
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:42,300
Yes. Right now, of course, there's one thing if you're going

255
00:09:42,300 --> 00:09:43,540
to see movies with non-economists,

256
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:44,580
and they'll just get mad at you.

257
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:45,860
Then there's a reason to sit through it.

258
00:09:46,260 --> 00:09:48,420
But if you're all by, how many of you,

259
00:09:48,420 --> 00:09:50,220
so this is actually not technically predicted

260
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:52,460
by economics, but I feel like economists ought to do it.

261
00:09:52,460 --> 00:09:54,020
I feel like economists ought to do this.

262
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:55,540
See movies alone.

263
00:09:56,140 --> 00:09:57,540
Yes, it's not that social.

264
00:09:57,540 --> 00:09:58,860
Like, why don't you just go by yourself

265
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:00,420
and see the movie you want when you want to see it?

266
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:01,900
And when you're with other people,

267
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:03,180
do something with the other people.

268
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:04,140
All right.

269
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:06,300
So, you know, or maybe you want to talk about the movie you

270
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:07,260
for or after or whatever.

271
00:10:07,260 --> 00:10:10,780
But still, there I think there is a stigma

272
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:14,340
that economists must Vulcan mind meld to overcome.

273
00:10:14,900 --> 00:10:15,180
All right.

274
00:10:16,060 --> 00:10:18,620
So, Tyler talked, here I will thank Tyler.

275
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:20,060
Tyler got me to start seeing movies alone,

276
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:21,020
start walking out of movies.

277
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:22,100
I'm eternally grateful.

278
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:22,420
It's great.

279
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:23,260
Okay.

280
00:10:23,580 --> 00:10:26,140
It's Tyler Cowan made you antisocial.

281
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:30,580
Well, made me fit for a good society.

282
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:35,540
A society we don't have yet, but one day we'll because of what he

283
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:36,820
said to me that one day.

284
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:38,780
But everything is going to fall into place.

285
00:10:40,540 --> 00:10:42,300
Now, we're going to go over some

286
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,580
of the main documented anomalies.

287
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:46,260
But first, I'm just going to pre-answer some

288
00:10:46,260 --> 00:10:47,540
of the blanket objections.

289
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:51,380
So, these are arguments that economists often make to say,

290
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:53,540
look, why do I not even have to read this garbage?

291
00:10:54,420 --> 00:10:56,740
Why do I not need to even open up the textbook

292
00:10:56,740 --> 00:10:57,740
to see what's inside?

293
00:10:57,820 --> 00:11:01,540
Why can I just pre-dismiss it all so I don't even have

294
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:03,900
to judge it case by case on whether the evidence is any good?

295
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:07,460
I can just say that it's ruled out on methodological grounds.

296
00:11:08,380 --> 00:11:08,740
All right.

297
00:11:09,660 --> 00:11:10,580
So, what are they?

298
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:15,700
First one, this is where the economist plays philosopher

299
00:11:15,700 --> 00:11:16,220
of science.

300
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:18,180
So, you start hearing about anomalies.

301
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:20,740
You say, well, look, all theories are false.

302
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:23,100
What matters is prediction.

303
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:26,980
So, where does this all theories are false stuff come from?

304
00:11:26,980 --> 00:11:31,820
Yeah, Friedman and, you know, so, you know, so,

305
00:11:31,820 --> 00:11:33,580
Popper is kind of in there.

306
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:35,900
I actually think Popper is to blame too.

307
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:37,820
Even, you know, somebody said, well, that's kind of weird.

308
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:39,340
He's the father of falsificationism.

309
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:42,300
So, the idea is that, like, every theory is going

310
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:43,900
to have problems, but, and we just keep,

311
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:45,740
we have a theory that's going to have problems.

312
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:48,100
We have a theory until you show me the problem,

313
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:48,900
that's the best we can do.

314
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:50,420
Everything's going to fail eventually.

315
00:11:50,860 --> 00:11:52,980
But if you haven't found a problem yet,

316
00:11:52,980 --> 00:11:54,420
that's the best that we can expect.

317
00:11:55,100 --> 00:11:55,340
All right.

318
00:11:56,140 --> 00:11:56,900
All theories are false.

319
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:57,940
What matters is prediction.

320
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:01,500
So, in one reply is, there's plenty of,

321
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,900
plenty of the anomalies evidence is showing the predictions

322
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:04,220
are wrong.

323
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:09,100
So, in that case, the objection is just really not,

324
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:10,900
is just really not relevant.

325
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:12,900
And on top of it, this all theories

326
00:12:12,900 --> 00:12:15,620
are false idea is strange in itself.

327
00:12:15,620 --> 00:12:18,140
You might say, well, all theories are false

328
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:20,780
to if you demand on accuracy to an infinite number

329
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:21,740
of decimal places.

330
00:12:23,140 --> 00:12:26,140
Fine. But what if your theory is false

331
00:12:26,140 --> 00:12:27,460
to the first decimal place?

332
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:31,060
What if, like, the first digit is completely wrong?

333
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:34,500
Right? You know, so, and, you know, of course,

334
00:12:34,780 --> 00:12:37,420
very often people actually, when they're thinking

335
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:38,660
about whether a theory is relevant,

336
00:12:38,660 --> 00:12:39,500
you have to say, well,

337
00:12:39,780 --> 00:12:42,900
does the real world even closely match the assumptions?

338
00:12:43,340 --> 00:12:45,540
So, for example, suppose that someone wanted to show

339
00:12:45,540 --> 00:12:47,140
that perfect competition was wrong.

340
00:12:47,500 --> 00:12:49,380
And the way you do it is you go and test the model

341
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:53,700
on say government supply of water.

342
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:54,740
When government supply, you know,

343
00:12:54,740 --> 00:12:56,860
like a monopoly water company.

344
00:12:56,860 --> 00:12:58,780
And then you say, see, perfect competition's wrong.

345
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:02,500
Right? What's the obvious thing to say in defense

346
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:04,380
of the perfectly competitive model if you test it

347
00:13:04,740 --> 00:13:07,420
on the monopoly water company?

348
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:12,820
Yes. It's not a theory about what monopolies do.

349
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:15,660
It's a theory about what competitors do.

350
00:13:15,700 --> 00:13:18,100
So, no matter what you're saying,

351
00:13:18,100 --> 00:13:20,660
actually there's always some effort to see, well,

352
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:23,940
do the basic assumptions of the model seem more or less right?

353
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:27,260
Milton Friedman actually has this line where he says, look,

354
00:13:27,660 --> 00:13:31,500
the reason why Newtonian physics works isn't

355
00:13:31,500 --> 00:13:33,380
that the world is approximately a vacuum.

356
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:35,060
No one's reading it.

357
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:36,380
Yes, it is.

358
00:13:36,940 --> 00:13:37,940
Of course that's why.

359
00:13:38,260 --> 00:13:42,260
You know, if we're doing Newtonian physics in oil,

360
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:45,140
then things don't fall according to that equation.

361
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:49,900
So, why is it that objects fall very close,

362
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:52,740
almost as Newton says they will through air?

363
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:55,780
Because air, there's hardly any air actually.

364
00:13:55,780 --> 00:13:57,100
We're almost in a vacuum.

365
00:13:58,820 --> 00:13:59,940
Right? You know, like one atmosphere

366
00:13:59,940 --> 00:14:01,980
of pressure is almost no pressure actually.

367
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:05,420
So, this is not a class in philosophy science.

368
00:14:05,420 --> 00:14:06,500
I'm not going to test you on this.

369
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:10,580
But, yeah, worth pointing out that the fact

370
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,420
that Friedman said it, you know, like how much did Friedman really

371
00:14:12,420 --> 00:14:13,500
know about philosophy of science?

372
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:16,540
It seems like he just sort of read a few things and said,

373
00:14:16,540 --> 00:14:18,860
well, now all economists have to go

374
00:14:18,860 --> 00:14:20,580
with this one controversial philosophy,

375
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:22,500
which apparently is the only one I read about.

376
00:14:23,340 --> 00:14:24,700
You know, you can be a super smart guy,

377
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:28,180
but if you only read one out of a dozen different views,

378
00:14:28,700 --> 00:14:30,820
the fact that you adopted is really not that convincing.

379
00:14:32,300 --> 00:14:32,660
All right.

380
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:35,900
Now, second objection to the anomaly say, look,

381
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:38,580
sure there's all kinds of random error, but it all cancels out.

382
00:14:38,580 --> 00:14:41,660
And the standard answer is no, they don't cancel out.

383
00:14:42,100 --> 00:14:43,980
The errors are actually systematic.

384
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:45,460
There's a pattern of error,

385
00:14:46,020 --> 00:14:48,460
such as people don't walk out of movies.

386
00:14:49,380 --> 00:14:51,540
Right? It's not like sometimes people are really enjoying a

387
00:14:51,540 --> 00:14:53,140
movie and they randomly walk out.

388
00:14:53,460 --> 00:14:55,140
And other times they're really hating a movie

389
00:14:55,140 --> 00:14:56,340
and they randomly stay in.

390
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:58,420
Instead, there seems to be a general pattern

391
00:14:58,420 --> 00:15:01,180
that people are not enjoying a movie, sit in there watching it

392
00:15:01,660 --> 00:15:03,220
because they already paid their money.

393
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:04,940
All right.

394
00:15:04,940 --> 00:15:07,580
And, of course, if that argument no longer makes any sense

395
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:08,820
to you, congratulations.

396
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:11,180
So you are ready for your PhD in economics.

397
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:14,460
But if it still is of appeal to you, then you still have

398
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:18,780
to burn out that last remnant of humanity and just say, no.

399
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:22,340
It is ridiculous to sit there suffering

400
00:15:22,340 --> 00:15:24,820
through a movie you don't like just because you already paid.

401
00:15:25,540 --> 00:15:26,340
Yeah, Ash?

402
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:28,340
Yeah, to me what makes it even less sense than sitting

403
00:15:28,340 --> 00:15:30,020
through a whole movie is reading a whole book.

404
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:33,940
Right? A movie is over in a couple hours.

405
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:35,020
Yeah, yeah, sure.

406
00:15:35,020 --> 00:15:42,060
I think a lot more people don't finish books.

407
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:45,340
Although also books are much less likely to be starved.

408
00:15:45,340 --> 00:15:46,900
I don't know.

409
00:15:47,620 --> 00:15:48,020
All right.

410
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:49,700
And then a final objection.

411
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:52,900
This one says anomalies arise purely due to weak incentives.

412
00:15:53,620 --> 00:15:54,580
And, indeed, it's true

413
00:15:54,580 --> 00:15:56,180
that in most psychology experiments there are no

414
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:57,260
monetary incentives.

415
00:15:57,820 --> 00:16:00,820
And in economics experiments,

416
00:16:01,020 --> 00:16:02,740
often the incentives are very weak.

417
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:05,740
So oftentimes there are experiments where all

418
00:16:05,740 --> 00:16:07,380
that happens is that if you do well,

419
00:16:07,380 --> 00:16:08,580
then your name is entered in the lottery.

420
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:10,780
So only one person in the entire room even gets paid.

421
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:13,260
So you might say, oh, well, people are acting this way

422
00:16:13,260 --> 00:16:15,060
because of weak incentives.

423
00:16:15,660 --> 00:16:18,500
So now a reply that is often made, this is one

424
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:19,860
that I think actually is overstated,

425
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:21,180
but at least it's a common reply,

426
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:23,620
is that strong incentives often don't matter.

427
00:16:24,620 --> 00:16:25,900
Furthermore, many people said a lot

428
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:27,620
of the big anomalies appear in financial markets

429
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:29,540
where it seems like there's actually huge incentives

430
00:16:29,980 --> 00:16:30,620
to get it right.

431
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:32,020
And yet people keep doing it wrong.

432
00:16:32,020 --> 00:16:36,100
Right now a point that I like to push is even

433
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:38,420
if this is only true, these results are still really

434
00:16:38,420 --> 00:16:41,820
important because actually we make a lot of choices

435
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:43,820
that have very weak incentives.

436
00:16:44,660 --> 00:16:46,340
Most obviously for voting.

437
00:16:47,900 --> 00:16:49,660
Voting is a choice that we make

438
00:16:49,660 --> 00:16:51,220
that has very big social implications,

439
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:52,540
but there's very little incentive

440
00:16:52,540 --> 00:16:53,940
to vote one way versus the other.

441
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:57,300
So even if you thought that weak incentives changed behavior,

442
00:16:57,700 --> 00:16:59,580
there are a number of kinds of behavior

443
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:00,540
where the incentives are weak.

444
00:17:00,540 --> 00:17:02,900
So in that case the behavioral economics should give us a

445
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:05,380
better prediction of behavior than the regular stuff.

446
00:17:05,740 --> 00:17:08,260
Or use another one I like, how about charitable donation?

447
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:11,220
What selfish incentive do you have to go

448
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:13,100
and carefully research charities to make sure

449
00:17:13,100 --> 00:17:15,180
that the world is getting the most bang for your buck?

450
00:17:17,180 --> 00:17:17,940
Not much.

451
00:17:18,620 --> 00:17:23,100
Right? So if weak incentives lead to bad behavior,

452
00:17:23,420 --> 00:17:26,380
then what might you think that philanthropists will do

453
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:28,020
when they go in and hand out their money?

454
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:30,620
Or fail to do is a better way.

455
00:17:30,620 --> 00:17:34,340
What would philanthropists generally fail to do?

456
00:17:35,900 --> 00:17:39,020
Research. And there's actually a whole movement dedicated

457
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:42,540
to trying to improve this behavior called effective?

458
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:43,300
Alterism.

459
00:17:43,300 --> 00:17:44,260
Effective alterism.

460
00:17:44,780 --> 00:17:46,300
Right? Where they look at the world and they say, look,

461
00:17:46,300 --> 00:17:48,300
it seems like there's a lot of charitable donation,

462
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:51,100
the dollars are massive, but the accomplishment is small

463
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:54,060
because people spend so much money on tons of stupid stuff.

464
00:17:54,060 --> 00:17:56,740
So at least it's an interesting thing to consider.

465
00:17:56,740 --> 00:17:59,340
Maybe people don't really research it in the same way

466
00:17:59,340 --> 00:18:01,020
that they would research their portfolio

467
00:18:01,380 --> 00:18:03,580
because the main reason people give

468
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:05,900
to charity perhaps is not actually to accomplish good,

469
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:06,980
but to feel like they're doing good.

470
00:18:06,980 --> 00:18:08,500
And you can get the feeling of doing good

471
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:09,820
without doing any research at all.

472
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:12,860
In fact, it seems like one of the best predictors

473
00:18:12,860 --> 00:18:14,980
of who you'll give charity to is just who other people you

474
00:18:14,980 --> 00:18:16,020
respect are giving money to.

475
00:18:16,940 --> 00:18:21,500
So like in New York City, there is a whole society

476
00:18:21,500 --> 00:18:25,900
of philanthropists and, you know, I'm only a messenger.

477
00:18:25,900 --> 00:18:28,700
From what I understand, it is dominated by the wives

478
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:30,900
and other female relatives of the richest men in the city.

479
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:35,300
Right? And they will come to decisions,

480
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:37,420
what are the most important charities in the world?

481
00:18:37,820 --> 00:18:39,700
And their ideas about what the most important charities

482
00:18:39,700 --> 00:18:42,660
in the world are based upon being a New York socialite seem

483
00:18:42,660 --> 00:18:44,060
to be very much at odds

484
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:46,060
with what any cost-benefit analysis would ever say.

485
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:47,740
It's like, well, it's very important

486
00:18:47,740 --> 00:18:48,660
to support New York Ballet.

487
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:49,180
That's a given.

488
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:51,460
Right? And New York City is a very important place

489
00:18:51,460 --> 00:18:54,020
and that's like the most pressing concern

490
00:18:54,020 --> 00:18:55,620
in the world is Exhibition Ballet.

491
00:18:55,940 --> 00:18:59,380
And then, let's see, really important to go

492
00:18:59,380 --> 00:19:00,740
and have more money for breast cancer.

493
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:04,180
Like, well, how much do we already have per person here

494
00:19:04,420 --> 00:19:05,820
compared to a bunch of other diseases

495
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:08,780
that kill a lot more people but don't get the same kind

496
00:19:08,780 --> 00:19:10,580
of funding and so on down the list.

497
00:19:10,580 --> 00:19:12,100
So it seems like there may be a problem there.

498
00:19:13,340 --> 00:19:13,540
All right.

499
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:15,780
Now anyway, so during the next two lectures,

500
00:19:15,780 --> 00:19:17,100
I'm going to be playing devil's advocate.

501
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,660
So I will in many ways just be channeling Conman

502
00:19:20,660 --> 00:19:23,660
and Tversky who are the most famous behavioral economists

503
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:25,300
in history, even today.

504
00:19:25,580 --> 00:19:27,900
Thaler, by virtue of staying alive,

505
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:29,860
I think is gradually pushing out Tversky.

506
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:34,220
But Conman is still hanging in there and still going strong.

507
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:38,940
So anyway, so now a strange thing

508
00:19:38,940 --> 00:19:40,060
about behavioral economics is

509
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:42,780
that there generally are bimodal reactions to it.

510
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:45,340
So there's either the view of it's worthless

511
00:19:45,340 --> 00:19:47,540
and I don't ever have to read it or the view

512
00:19:47,540 --> 00:19:50,140
of it's the only thing worth reading and anyone

513
00:19:50,140 --> 00:19:54,020
and it means that pretty much everything else that's ever gone

514
00:19:54,020 --> 00:19:55,660
on in economics is almost worthless

515
00:19:56,180 --> 00:19:58,940
because behavioral economics has corrected it all.

516
00:19:58,940 --> 00:20:19,940
All right.


